1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Let's talk about credentials.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Dev keys and prod keys look the same,

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
but they have to be separated.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Blurring them is how a small mistake becomes a big one.

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
This lesson covers how Cloud Code authenticates,

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
the order it picks credentials in,

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
and how CI gets its own credential.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Let's break it down.

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Cloud Code can authenticate a few different ways,

10
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
and it helps to know the main three by name.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
The first is a direct API key.

12
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
That's anthropic API key, sent as a header.

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
And it's the common choice in development,

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
set as an environment variable on your machine.

15
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The second is API key helper,

16
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
a script that returns a short-lived key,

17
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,000
usually pulled from a vault.

18
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,000
That's the pattern you reach for

19
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
when production secrets rotate.

20
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The third is setup token,

21
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
a command that mints a long-lived OAuth token

22
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
for continuous integration,

23
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
where there's no interactive browser to log in with.

24
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Each fits a different situation.

25
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
A key for local dev, a helper for rotating prod secrets,

26
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
and a token for automation.

27
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Keep those roles distinct,

28
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
and the rest of this lesson lines up.

29
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Here's the core discipline.

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Development and production get separate keys, always.

31
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Your dev key is a low-scope credential

32
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
that only ever touches test data and sandboxes.

33
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:23,000
That's deliberate.

34
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,000
You design its scope,

35
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
so a leak can reach nothing in production,

36
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
and you verify the scope actually enforces that,

37
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
leaving no path from that key to real user data.

38
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Your prod key is a different animal.

39
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
It's tightly scoped, it rotates,

40
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
it gets pulled from a vault at runtime,

41
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
and it reaches real production data.

42
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And critically, it is never shared back down to dev.

43
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
The reason to keep them apart is containment.

44
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Two keys mean two separate blast radii.

45
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
When the two environments share a single credential,

46
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
a leak anywhere becomes a leak everywhere.

47
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
And that's exactly the outcome separation prevents.

48
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Let me make the payoff concrete,

49
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
because this is the whole reason you bother.

50
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
The point of separate keys is to contain a single leak.

51
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
When your dev key leaks and dev keys leak more often

52
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
because they're on laptops and in test scripts,

53
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
that key is designed to reach nothing in production,

54
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
and you've verified the scope actually enforces that.

55
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Each environment carries its own blast radius,

56
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
so an incident in one stays boxed inside that one.

57
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Separation also gives you operational freedom.

58
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
You can rotate or revoke the dev key

59
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:36,000
without breaking production,

60
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
and rotate prod without disrupting your developers.

61
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Compare that to one shared key spanning both.

62
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Now a single leak exposes everything at once,

63
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
and you can't rotate it

64
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
without taking down work on both sides.

65
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
One key, one big problem.

66
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
When Cloud Code has several credentials available,

67
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:58,000
it doesn't guess.

68
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
It follows a defined precedence order,

69
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
and knowing the top of that order

70
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
clears up a common misconception.

71
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
People assume Anthropic API key always wins. It doesn't.

72
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Cloud provider credentials come first.

73
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
If the flags for Amazon Bedrock, Google Vertex,

74
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
or Foundry are set, those win.

75
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Next comes Anthropic Auth token,

76
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
a bearer token used for an LLM gateway or proxy.

77
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Only below that sits Anthropic API key,

78
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
the direct API header,

79
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
and beneath the API key fall the API key helper output

80
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
and the CI OAuth token.

81
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So the direct key is in the middle of the stack, not the top.

82
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
If you ever get surprised by which credential is in use,

83
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
walk the order from the top down.

84
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Continuous integration is a special case

85
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,000
because there's no person sitting there

86
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
to log in through a browser.

87
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
So CI gets its own credential.

88
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
The setup token command mints a long-lived OAuth token

89
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
built for exactly this.

90
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Now, here's the part people miss.

91
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
It prints the token to your screen and saves it nowhere.

92
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Nothing is written to disk for you.

93
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
It's your job to copy that value

94
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
and set it as the Cloud Code OAuth token

95
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
environment variable in your CI system,

96
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
ideally in that system's own secret storage.

97
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
And this token is scoped to inference only.

98
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
So even if it leaks,

99
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
it can't be turned around to do more than run model calls.

100
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
A purpose-built, narrowly scoped credential for automation

101
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
and one you handle deliberately

102
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
rather than one that quietly persists.

103
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Let's talk about rotation in production

104
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
because this is where short-lived credentials

105
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,000
earn their keep.

106
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
A short-lived token is one that expires quickly

107
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
and that expiry is a feature.

108
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,000
If someone steals it,

109
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
it's useless within minutes rather than months.

110
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The trouble with short lifetimes

111
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
is that something has to keep fetching fresh tokens.

112
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
And that's exactly what API Key Helper does.

113
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
It refetches the key on a timer

114
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
or after an authentication failure.

115
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
So rotation just happens with no redeploy and no code change.

116
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
And the mental model to lock in is this.

117
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:19,000
You rotate at the source,

118
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
you change the secret in the vault,

119
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and every run afterward picks up the new one automatically.

120
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Short lifetimes plus automatic refresh

121
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
is how production secrets stay fresh

122
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
without anyone babysitting them.

123
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
OneTrap is worth calling out on its own

124
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
because it's a classic and it's costly.

125
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Never commit the credentials file.

126
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
The whole design is that this store

127
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
is protected by the operating system.

128
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,000
A locked local file or the system key chain.

129
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
And it isn't meant to be a repository artifact.

130
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
If you commit it, you hand your token to everyone

131
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
with access to that repo and to get history forever.

132
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,000
So the split is clean.

133
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Secrets live in the store or a vault.

134
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
And your code lives in Git and the two never mix.

135
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
You're getting your should keep that file out by default.

136
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
And if a secret ever does slip into a commit,

137
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
don't just delete the line.

138
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Treat it as leaked, rotate it immediately,

139
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
and scrub it from history.

140
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Let's look at how the environment itself limits damage.

141
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Because good architecture assumes a credential

142
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
will eventually be exposed.

143
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Scoped credentials are the first idea.

144
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Rather than one powerful key,

145
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
a cloud session uses narrow, short-lived credentials,

146
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
each limited to a single purpose

147
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
and expiring on its own timeline.

148
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
If one is compromised, it unlocks very little.

149
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
The second idea is that credentials stay put.

150
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
In the sandbox model,

151
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
they never leave the environment they run in.

152
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
So they can't be exfiltrated out to somewhere else.

153
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
And third is token translation.

154
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
A proxy swaps a scoped sandbox credential

155
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
for the real token behind the scenes,

156
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
which means the real powerful token

157
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
is never exposed to the code

158
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
running in the sandbox at all.

159
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Layer those, and a single leak

160
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
buys an attacker almost nothing.

161
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Here's the bottom line.

162
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Splitting dev and prod keys keeps one leak

163
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
from becoming two leaks.

164
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
A leaked dev key never reaches production.

165
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Anthropic API key is not the top of the precedence order.

166
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:31,000
And setup token mints a CI token, it saves nowhere.

167
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Just remember, API key helper

168
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
rotates prod secrets from a vault,

169
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
and you never commit their credential file.

170
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
That wraps up separating dev and prod credentials.

171
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Let's keep going in the next video.

